    ks.name, ByteBufferUtil.bytes(ut.getTypeName()), fieldNames, fieldTypes));
```
Reason:
The `loadUserTypes` method iterates over the `udts` collection without checking if it is empty. If `udts` is empty, the loop does not execute, but subsequent logic still assumes that there are user-defined types to process, leading to errors when trying to load types from an empty collection.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if (udts.isEmpty()) {
    return;
}
```
```java
loadUserTypes(notLoaded, ks);
```
Reason:
The added check for `udts.isEmpty()` allows the method to return early if there are no user-defined types to process, preventing any further logic from executing that assumes the presence of UDTs. This ensures that the loader can handle keyspaces with zero user types without failing.

